<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Vladislaus III, Duke of Bohemia</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. <a href="/years/1222">1222</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> · Also known as Vladislaus Henry, Vladislav Jindřich · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/premyslid-dynasty">Přemyslid dynasty</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Vladislaus Henry, a Přemyslid prince, was briefly Duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bohemia">Bohemia</a> in 1197 as Vladislaus III before giving up the throne to his brother Ottokar I in return for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moravia">Moravia</a>. As Margrave of Moravia from 1197 until his death in 1222 he united the former Moravian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/appanage">appanages</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brno">Brno</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/olomouc">Olomouc</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/znojmo">Znojmo</a>, ending their separate princely lines and tying Moravia more closely to the Bohemian crown.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/vladislaus-iii-duke-of-bohemia.jpg" alt="Vladislaus III, Duke of Bohemia" width="320" height="320" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: August Sedláček · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:PecetVladislavaJindricha.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Vladislaus Henry was born around 1160, the second son of Duke <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vladislaus-ii-of-bohemia">Vladislaus II of Bohemia</a> by his second wife, Judith of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thuringia">Thuringia</a>, and so the younger brother of Ottokar I. In the struggles within the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/premyslid-dynasty">Přemyslid dynasty</a> he supported Ottokar when he seized the Bohemian duchy in 1192, and Ottokar, with the consent of Emperor Henry VI, made him margrave of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moravia">Moravia</a>; he had been Prince of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brno">Brno</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/znojmo">Znojmo</a> since 1191. The brothers never paid the sum the emperor demanded, and in June 1193 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imperial-diet">Imperial Diet</a> at Worms deposed them in favour of their cousin, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prague">Prague</a> bishop Henry Bretislaus. Vladislaus spent the following years at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prague-castle">Prague Castle</a> under the duke's suspicious eye.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>After the deaths of Henry VI and Bretislaus in 1197 he was released and elected duke on 22 June, with his brother present. Faced with a rising in Ottokar's favour, he abdicated on 6 December. The two armies were encamped facing each other when the brothers met and agreed that Ottokar would take the throne, though without imperial approval, and Vladislaus the hereditary title of a near-autonomous <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moravia">Moravia</a>. His renunciation helped to end the destructive dynastic wars of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/premyslid-dynasty">Přemyslids</a>, which had lasted more than twenty-five years since their father's death, and he was the last ruler of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bohemia">Bohemia</a> to hold the title of duke.</p><p>In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moravia">Moravia</a> he worked closely with his brother. He had the rival claimant Prince Spytihněv III of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brno">Brno</a> blinded and installed his own confessor, Daniel Milík, as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">Bishop</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prague">Prague</a>, the first investiture by a reigning prince in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bohemia">Bohemia</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/golden-bull-of-sicily">Golden Bull of Sicily</a> of 1212 acknowledged his status as a Prince of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-roman-empire">Holy Roman Empire</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In 1205 Vladislaus founded the Cistercian abbey of Velehrad as the family monastery and burial place of the Moravian margraves. He married a woman named Heilwida, or Hedwig, of unknown origin, and died childless at his court in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/znojmo">Znojmo</a> on 12 August 1222. He was buried at Velehrad, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moravia">Moravia</a> passed back to King Ottokar I, who granted it to his younger son Vladislaus II.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Vladislaus III of Bohemia” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Vladislaus Henry</b>, brother of King Ottokar I, governed it as margrave from 1197 to 1222 and absorbed the old appanages, so that by the early thirteenth century the separate principalities had merged into a single land with its own nobility, courts and sense of identity.</blockquote><a href="/empire/margraviate-of-moravia">Margraviate of Moravia · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Around 1182 Moravia was declared a margraviate directly subject to the emperor, and by about 1197 <b>Vladislaus Henry</b> united the Moravian lands under his rule.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-brno">Duchy of Brno</a></li><li><blockquote>Moravia became a margraviate under imperial recognition, and around 1197 <b>Vladislaus Henry</b> united it.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-olomouc">Duchy of Olomouc</a></li><li><blockquote>The appanage system ended in the late 12th century as Moravia became a margraviate; around 1197 <b>Vladislaus Henry</b> united the Moravian lands.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-znojmo">Duchy of Znojmo</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prague">Prague</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/czech-republic">the Czech Republic</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/znojmo">Znojmo</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/czech-republic">the Czech Republic</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=50.09,14.42&amp;year=1212" aria-label="Prague on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4880 422 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Vladislaus III, Duke of Bohemia"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5330 692h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5330 692h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=50.09,14.42&amp;year=1212">What was here in 1212? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
